Test Group 1: Automated Triggers
Test Case 1.1: Time-Period Trigger

Objective: Validate that a punch sequence initiates when the countdown interval expires.

Steps:

Set the system to Run mode.

Navigate to the Configuration page and set a short interval for testing.

Observe the main screen countdown timer.

Wait for the interval to reach 0.00.

Expected Result: The system logs "Time Interval Reached", updates the reason display to "Time", and transitions the FSM to begin the punch sequence.

Test Case 1.2: Temperature-Level Trigger

Objective: Validate that a punch sequence initiates when the must temperature exceeds the set point and the dwell time is satisfied.

Steps:

Set the system to Run mode.

Set TempSetPoint below the current ambient temperature reading.

Ensure TempDwellTime has exceeded the minimum wait period.

Expected Result: The system logs the thermal trigger, updates the reason display to "Temp", resets the dwell timer, and triggers the punch sequence.

Test Group 2: Sequence and Timing Accuracy
Test Case 2.1: Full Sequential Operation & Shake Delay

Objective: Validate that all four actuators fire sequentially using the strict 5s/15s timing and that the shake routine waits for the final actuator's fixed StrokeUpTime to conclude.

Steps:

Configure StrokeDownTime in a 5-second integer step (e.g., 5s, 10s, 30s).

Set ShakeDelay to 10 seconds.

Initiate a punch sequence.

Monitor the four output pins.

Expected Results:

Pin 1 goes HIGH for the configured StrokeDownTime (e.g., 30s), then LOW for exactly 15s.

Pin 2 goes HIGH for StrokeDownTime, then LOW for exactly 15s.

Pin 3 goes HIGH for StrokeDownTime, then LOW for exactly 15s.

Pin 4 goes HIGH for StrokeDownTime, then LOW for exactly 15s.

Crucial Validation: No pins flutter or enter the rapid shake cycle until exactly 10 seconds after Pin 4 has completed its 15-second upstroke.

Test Group 3: Non-Blocking & UI Responsiveness
Test Case 3.1: UI Interaction During Active Actuation

Objective: Validate that the display and touch matrix remain completely unblocked during FSM execution.

Steps:

Trigger a manual punch sequence.

While Actuator 2 is moving (mid-sequence), tap the screen to navigate to the Info or Config page.

Rapidly tap a configuration increment/decrement button.

Expected Result: The screen changes pages instantly, and buttons respond without stutter, lag, or skipped touches, proving blocking logic has been eradicated.

Test Group 4: State Control & Interlocks
Test Case 4.1: Run/Pause Independence from Manual Cycle

Objective: Validate that a manual cycle operates correctly regardless of the automated state.

Steps:

Ensure the system is in Pause/Stop mode and trigger a manual cycle.

Wait for completion.

Set the system to Run mode and trigger a manual cycle.

Expected Result: The manual cycle executes flawlessly in both states without double-triggering or glitching the underlying interval counters.

Test Case 4.2: Manual Cycle Interaction with Run-Cycling Toggle

Objective: Validate that the main Run/Cycle button can act as an abort/toggle during a manual sequence.

Steps:

Initiate a manual punch cycle.

While the cycle is active, press the on-screen "Cycle" / "Run-Stop" toggle button.

Press the button a second time.

Expected Result: The first press immediately triggers AbortPunch(), safely dropping all actuator pins LOW and halting the main sequence. The second press registers cleanly and initiates the appropriate state based on your UI logic.

Test Group 5: Power Failure, EEPROM Retention & Program Continuance
Test Case 5.1: EEPROM Parameter Data Retention Across Hard Reset

Objective: Validate that critical configuration values (temperatures, stroke times, and total cycle counts) persist across power cycles.

Steps:

Set specific non-default values via the UI: TempSetPoint = 85°F, StrokeDownTime = 25s, SetTimeRep_UI = 3, AutoCycleEnabled = 1 (Running).

Disconnect main power to the board completely for 30 seconds.

Re-apply power and observe boot values on the UI and Serial monitor.

Expected Result: EEPROM restores AutoCycleEnabled, TempSetPoint, StrokeDownTime, and accumulated Cycles accurately without reverting to magic-byte fallback defaults.

Test Case 5.2: Mid-Cycle Power Outage Recovery ("Power Failure Punch")

Objective: Validate that if power fails while the unit is active and armed, it automatically triggers a recovery punch sequence upon reboot to ensure the cap remains wet.

Steps:

Set system to Run mode (AutoCycleEnabled = 1).

Allow system uptime to exceed 1 hour (or mock EEPROM.read(5) == 1).

Cut main power abruptly mid-cycle while an actuator is down.

Restore power to the Arduino Mega.

Expected Result: During setup(), the system detects EEPROM.read(5) == 1 and AutoCycleEnabled == 1. It logs "Power fail so punch once now", sets s_PunchReason = "Power", and immediately executes a single recovery punch sequence.

Test Case 5.3: Mandatory Shake on Partial Cycle Abort

Objective: Validate that if a cycle is interrupted, all solenoids immediately release and the mandatory shake routine runs to clear cap debris before returning to idle.

Steps:

Initiate a sequence.

Mid-way through the sequence, trigger an abort condition.

Expected Result: All actuator pins instantly drop LOW to prevent mechanical bind. Upon system recovery, the state machine forces the rapid down/up shake toggles across the hardware before safely settling into an idle state.